¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙØAnd it came to pass on the morrow, that the evil spirit from God came upon Saul, and he prophesied in the midst of the house: and David played with his hand, as at other times: and there was a javelin in Saul's hand.¦layout ªlast_verse
„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd Saul cast the javelin; for he said, I will smite David even to the wall with it. And David avoided out of his presence twice.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙXAnd Saul was afraid of David, because the LORD was with him, and was departed from Saul.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}Therefore Saul removed him from him, and made him his captain over a thousand; and he went out and came in before the people.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙLAnd David behaved himself wisely in all his ways; and the LORD was with him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRWherefore when Saul saw that he behaved himself very wisely, he was afraid of him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRBut all Israel and Judah loved David, because he went out and came in before them.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙïAnd Saul said to David, Behold my elder daughter Merab, her will I give thee to wife: only be thou valiant for me, and fight the LORD's battles. For Saul said, Let not mine hand be upon him, but let the hand of the Philistines be upon him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‚And David said unto Saul, Who am I? and what is my life, or my father's family in Israel, that I should be son in law to the king?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ“But it came to pass at the time when Merab Saul's daughter should have been given to David, that she was given unto Adriel the Meholathite to wife.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses˜„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVAnd Michal Saul's daughter loved David: and they told Saul, and the thing pleased him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙØAnd Saul said, I will give him her, that she may be a snare to him, and that the hand of the Philistines may be against him. Wherefore Saul said to David, Thou shalt this day be my son in law in the one of the twain.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ½And Saul commanded his servants, saying, Commune with David secretly, and say, Behold, the king hath delight in thee, and all his servants love thee: now therefore be the king's son in law.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¹And Saul's servants spake those words in the ears of David. And David said, Seemeth it to you a light thing to be a king's son in law, seeing that I am a poor man, and lightly esteemed?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFAnd the servants of Saul told him, saying, On this manner spake David.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙáAnd Saul said, Thus shall ye say to David, The king desireth not any dowry, but an hundred foreskins of the Philistines, to be avenged of the king's enemies. But Saul thought to make David fall by the hand of the Philistines.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd when his servants told David these words, it pleased David well to be the king's son in law: and the days were not expired.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚWherefore David arose and went, he and his men, and slew of the Philistines two hundred men; and David brought their foreskins, and they gave them in full tale to the king, that he might be the king's son in law. And Saul gave him Michal his daughter to wife.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ^And Saul saw and knew that the LORD was with David, and that Michal Saul's daughter loved him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙUAnd Saul was yet the more afraid of David; and Saul became David's enemy continually.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÃThen the princes of the Philistines went forth: and it came to pass, after they went forth, that David behaved himself more wisely than all the servants of Saul; so that his name was much set by.¦layout ªlast_verse